1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,183
Wednesday, the fourth of October, nineteen sixty-seven.

2
00:00:04,183 --> 00:00:14,333
Shortly after eleven at night, a row of lights falls out of a clear, moonless sky toward a fishing harbour in Nova Scotia, and goes into the sea.

3
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:18,104
One light stays behind, floating on the water.

4
00:00:18,104 --> 00:00:19,253
Police watch it.

5
00:00:19,253 --> 00:00:20,258
Then it sinks.

6
00:00:20,258 --> 00:00:21,839
The navy sends divers.

7
00:00:21,839 --> 00:00:25,933
They search for three days, and they find nothing at all.

8
00:00:26,399 --> 00:00:31,888
Canada's military then files the sighting as an unidentified flying object.

9
00:00:31,888 --> 00:00:35,766
That one word is why this is called Canada's Roswell.

10
00:00:36,233 --> 00:00:39,614
But here is the problem with every famous mystery.

11
00:00:39,614 --> 00:00:40,628
Stories travel.

12
00:00:40,628 --> 00:00:48,066
Tonight we follow this one link by link, from a payphone to a government file, and check what each hand added.

13
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:50,672
Facts first, theories labelled.

14
00:00:50,672 --> 00:00:51,845
Message received.

15
00:00:51,845 --> 00:00:52,466
Link one.

16
00:00:56,933 --> 00:00:57,783
Chapter one.

17
00:00:57,783 --> 00:00:58,420
The call.

18
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:07,133
Shag Harbour is a village of a few hundred people at the southern tip of Nova Scotia, on a coast built for fishing and fog.

19
00:01:07,599 --> 00:01:12,953
That night, five young people are driving home from a dance along Highway Three.

20
00:01:12,953 --> 00:01:16,433
At the wheel is Laurie Wickens, seventeen years old.

21
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:20,056
They see lights ahead, low over the water.

22
00:01:20,056 --> 00:01:22,311
Accounts differ on the number.

23
00:01:22,311 --> 00:01:23,439
Three, or four.

24
00:01:23,439 --> 00:01:24,716
Yellow, or white.

25
00:01:24,716 --> 00:01:28,400
All agree: a row, tilted, sinking toward the bay.

26
00:01:28,866 --> 00:01:32,688
Then a small hill blocks their view for about ten seconds.

27
00:01:32,688 --> 00:01:36,708
When they can see the water again, the row of lights is gone.

28
00:01:36,708 --> 00:01:40,266
In its place, one white light, sitting on the surface.

29
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:44,159
Wickens stops at a phone booth and calls the police.

30
00:01:44,159 --> 00:01:49,233
By his own later account, the first question is whether he has been drinking.

31
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,246
Then the payphone rings.

32
00:01:51,246 --> 00:01:53,308
The police are calling him back.

33
00:01:53,308 --> 00:01:55,177
Other people have called too.

34
00:01:55,177 --> 00:01:58,400
Something, they now realise, really did come down.

35
00:01:58,866 --> 00:02:01,823
Note what the first link actually carries.

36
00:02:01,823 --> 00:02:02,949
Not a spaceship.

37
00:02:02,949 --> 00:02:03,794
Not a crash.

38
00:02:03,794 --> 00:02:07,243
Just lights, a descent, and a light on the water.

39
00:02:07,243 --> 00:02:11,466
Everyone assumed the same thing: an aeroplane had gone down.

40
00:02:11,933 --> 00:02:17,630
Wickens died only this year, aged seventy-six, still the face of the story.

41
00:02:17,630 --> 00:02:18,997
Link one is solid.

42
00:02:18,997 --> 00:02:19,833
Pass it on.

43
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:21,119
Chapter two.

44
00:02:21,119 --> 00:02:21,803
The shore.

45
00:02:21,803 --> 00:02:28,430
A corporal and two constables from the Mounties reach the water within fifteen or twenty minutes.

46
00:02:28,430 --> 00:02:29,933
They expect survivors.

47
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:32,188
They are in time to see it.

48
00:02:32,188 --> 00:02:37,155
A light on the water, about half a mile out, drifting with the ebbing tide.

49
00:02:37,155 --> 00:02:39,407
It stays for roughly five minutes.

50
00:02:39,407 --> 00:02:40,533
Then it goes out.

51
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:50,239
Constable Ron O'Brien told reporters it was being carried out to sea by the tide, and was gone before a boat could reach it.

52
00:02:50,239 --> 00:02:51,580
Trained observers.

53
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:53,666
Same story as the teenagers.

54
00:02:54,133 --> 00:03:03,333
Within minutes, a Coast Guard lifeboat from Clark's Harbour and six or more local fishing boats are on the water, hunting for a downed aeroplane.

55
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:06,537
They find no wreckage and no survivors.

56
00:03:06,537 --> 00:03:13,697
They find a patch of foam, thirty to forty yards wide by one account, at least eighty feet by another.

57
00:03:13,697 --> 00:03:17,066
Fishermen thought it was not ordinary tide foam.

58
00:03:17,533 --> 00:03:24,035
Later retellings say the foam was yellow, bubbling, and smelled of sulphur, stretching half a mile.

59
00:03:24,035 --> 00:03:26,859
The reports from that week do not say that.

60
00:03:26,859 --> 00:03:28,633
It grows with each telling.

61
00:03:29,099 --> 00:03:30,482
Now the first crack.

62
00:03:30,482 --> 00:03:42,233
The Colorado university investigators, who telephoned the police weeks later, wrote a line the legend leaves out: apparently no one actually saw anything enter the water.

63
00:03:42,699 --> 00:03:45,912
One witness two miles away did see the descent.

64
00:03:45,912 --> 00:03:52,338
Three red-orange lights, moving at aircraft speed, with a whistling sound like a falling bomb.

65
00:03:52,338 --> 00:03:54,800
Then they dropped behind the houses.

66
00:03:55,266 --> 00:03:58,182
By three in the morning, the boats come home.

67
00:03:58,182 --> 00:04:02,263
Link two is strong on the lights and on the light that floated.

68
00:04:02,263 --> 00:04:04,466
It is weaker on the object itself.

69
00:04:04,933 --> 00:04:05,993
Chapter three.

70
00:04:05,993 --> 00:04:06,599
Halifax.

71
00:04:06,599 --> 00:04:13,037
At the Rescue Co-ordination Centre, the first job is simple: is any aircraft missing?

72
00:04:13,037 --> 00:04:15,233
By morning, the answer is no.

73
00:04:15,699 --> 00:04:19,958
At ten twenty that morning, a priority telex goes to Ottawa.

74
00:04:19,958 --> 00:04:28,333
It carries the police account: a dark object, over sixty feet long, with four white lights, whistling as it came down.

75
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:31,700
It adds two things the teenagers never said.

76
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:34,139
A bright flash when it hit the water.

77
00:04:34,139 --> 00:04:36,974
And a single light floating on the surface.

78
00:04:36,974 --> 00:04:40,666
Already, the story is bigger than the people who saw it.

79
00:04:41,133 --> 00:04:44,623
It also contains the phrase that makes history.

80
00:04:44,623 --> 00:04:50,266
All conventional explanations, aircraft, flares and the like, are dismissed.

81
00:04:50,266 --> 00:04:53,533
The message is therefore headed: UFO report.

82
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:01,429
The telex gives a position: forty-three degrees thirty and a half minutes north, sixty-five forty-five west.

83
00:05:01,429 --> 00:05:06,933
Just outside Lower Woods Harbour, about two hundred and seventy metres offshore.

84
00:05:07,399 --> 00:05:13,426
Here is what the Coast Guard cutter actually reported: many small boats, nil results.

85
00:05:13,426 --> 00:05:19,666
And, quote, all other possible leads, aircraft, flares, et cetera, checked, nil results.

86
00:05:20,133 --> 00:05:21,417
Remember that line.

87
00:05:21,417 --> 00:05:23,918
Nil results means they found nothing.

88
00:05:23,918 --> 00:05:27,162
It does not mean they proved it was not a flare.

89
00:05:27,162 --> 00:05:29,933
That difference will matter in two links.

90
00:05:30,399 --> 00:05:31,427
Chapter four.

91
00:05:31,427 --> 00:05:33,009
Ottawa and the Navy.

92
00:05:33,009 --> 00:05:40,600
The telex lands at the Air Force's air desk, the office that handles every UFO report in Canada.

93
00:05:41,066 --> 00:05:50,933
Squadron Leader Bain tells the press the Air Force gets hundreds of reports every week, but this is one of the few where it might get something concrete.

94
00:05:51,399 --> 00:05:53,914
He recommends an underwater search.

95
00:05:53,914 --> 00:05:56,932
The Navy tasks Fleet Diving Unit Atlantic.

96
00:05:56,932 --> 00:06:01,100
Two days after the sighting, divers reach Clark's Harbour.

97
00:06:01,566 --> 00:06:10,239
Seven divers eventually search an area about half a mile by a mile and a half, in water nine to twenty metres deep.

98
00:06:10,239 --> 00:06:10,993
Flat sand.

99
00:06:10,993 --> 00:06:12,200
Good visibility.

100
00:06:12,666 --> 00:06:14,625
They work through the weekend.

101
00:06:14,625 --> 00:06:22,066
If a sixty-foot machine, or even a large piece of one, had landed there, this is exactly the search that finds it.

102
00:06:22,533 --> 00:06:27,055
At six thirty on Sunday, the eighth of October, the search ends.

103
00:06:27,055 --> 00:06:32,566
A handwritten note in the file says it in four words: terminated, nil results.

104
00:06:33,033 --> 00:06:38,478
The Chronicle Herald reports it: not a trace, not a clue, not a bit of anything.

105
00:06:38,478 --> 00:06:45,966
In December, headquarters writes to two universities that the investigation ended without conclusive findings.

106
00:06:46,433 --> 00:06:53,966
One more detail: in nineteen sixty-seven, about one hundred and ninety UFO reports reached Ottawa.

107
00:06:53,966 --> 00:06:57,195
Only nine were judged worth investigating.

108
00:06:57,195 --> 00:06:58,809
Shag Harbour was one.

109
00:06:58,809 --> 00:07:01,500
So officials did take it seriously.

110
00:07:01,966 --> 00:07:02,841
Chapter five.

111
00:07:02,841 --> 00:07:03,850
The same night.

112
00:07:03,850 --> 00:07:08,491
Believers say that on the fourth of October, the whole region lit up.

113
00:07:08,491 --> 00:07:10,038
Other lights were seen.

114
00:07:10,038 --> 00:07:13,266
Here is the second place a message gets crowded.

115
00:07:13,733 --> 00:07:23,225
At seven fifteen, an Air Canada crew over Quebec reported a bright, rectangular object with trailing lights, and two silent explosions.

116
00:07:23,225 --> 00:07:26,600
That is hundreds of kilometres from Nova Scotia.

117
00:07:27,066 --> 00:07:35,151
Far out at sea, Captain Leo Mersey of the fishing boat Nickerson watched red flashing lights and four radar blips.

118
00:07:35,151 --> 00:07:40,966
Colorado's own notes put him about a hundred and seventy-five nautical miles away.

119
00:07:41,433 --> 00:07:46,759
In Halifax, around ten, someone phoned a newsroom about a glowing object.

120
00:07:46,759 --> 00:07:47,124
Fine.

121
00:07:47,124 --> 00:07:50,699
Different place, different hour, different light.

122
00:07:51,166 --> 00:07:55,705
Over the years, these reports were stitched into one night of UFOs.

123
00:07:55,705 --> 00:07:58,076
That makes the story feel stronger.

124
00:07:58,076 --> 00:08:03,699
It actually makes it weaker, because it hides which claim belongs to which witness.

125
00:08:04,166 --> 00:08:07,595
But there is a fair question about the explosions.

126
00:08:07,595 --> 00:08:14,933
A bright meteor, a bolide, often breaks into a row of lights as it falls, at a shallow angle, looking slow.

127
00:08:14,933 --> 00:08:18,500
That fits the first part of the Shag Harbour lights.

128
00:08:18,966 --> 00:08:23,113
It does not explain a light that floated on the water for minutes.

129
00:08:23,113 --> 00:08:25,500
A meteor is a good match for the fall.

130
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:27,699
It is a poor match for the landing.

131
00:08:28,166 --> 00:08:29,108
Chapter six.

132
00:08:29,108 --> 00:08:30,206
The lost link.

133
00:08:30,206 --> 00:08:39,464
Years later, the University of Colorado's UFO project, the Condon Committee, makes telephone calls about Shag Harbour.

134
00:08:39,464 --> 00:08:41,033
These notes survive.

135
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:48,843
The watch officer reads them a report in which the object, sixty feet long, exploded on hitting the water.

136
00:08:48,843 --> 00:08:53,000
The police corporal says no one actually saw anything go in.

137
00:08:53,466 --> 00:08:56,466
Two accounts from the same chain of command.

138
00:08:56,466 --> 00:08:58,306
One says a flash on impact.

139
00:08:58,306 --> 00:09:04,033
One says the light went out, and ten minutes later officers were rowing to the site.

140
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:07,365
The Colorado notes add another line.

141
00:09:07,365 --> 00:09:17,233
The nearby Air Force radar station reported no unusual radar object, and no Canadian or American air operations in the area.

142
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:21,643
Which brings us to the sceptics' favourite: marine flares.

143
00:09:21,643 --> 00:09:24,703
Boats fire them as standard distress signals.

144
00:09:24,703 --> 00:09:29,666
They ignite late, fall in a row, and float on the water as they burn out.

145
00:09:30,133 --> 00:09:37,998
Skeptoid's Brian Dunning argues a few flares, perhaps fired by a prankster, fit the descent and the floating light.

146
00:09:37,998 --> 00:09:42,033
He cannot prove it, and the file contains no matching boat.

147
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:47,508
Honest problem: the light reportedly lasted about five minutes on the water.

148
00:09:47,508 --> 00:09:53,899
Whether a single flare could burn that long, and then be carried by the tide, no one has settled.

149
00:09:54,366 --> 00:09:56,039
And now the famous memo.

150
00:09:56,039 --> 00:10:05,796
Colonel Turner writes that the search centre had, quote, discounted the possibility of aircraft, flares, floats, or any other known objects.

151
00:10:05,796 --> 00:10:08,933
That became: the government ruled out flares.

152
00:10:09,399 --> 00:10:11,688
Read it against the cutter's telex.

153
00:10:11,688 --> 00:10:18,098
The chain went from we checked and found nothing, to the government discounted every known object.

154
00:10:18,098 --> 00:10:19,732
A shrug became a verdict.

155
00:10:19,732 --> 00:10:21,433
That is the relay at work.

156
00:10:21,899 --> 00:10:22,876
Chapter seven.

157
00:10:22,876 --> 00:10:23,922
The added link.

158
00:10:23,922 --> 00:10:29,431
In nineteen ninety-three, a quarter of a century later, the story takes a turn.

159
00:10:29,431 --> 00:10:31,733
A book is published: Dark Object.

160
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:43,200
Its authors, UFO researchers Chris Styles and Don Ledger, interview old witnesses and promise the world's only government-documented UFO crash.

161
00:10:43,666 --> 00:10:46,311
Their new claim: the craft did not sink.

162
00:10:46,311 --> 00:10:56,033
It moved underwater, seventy kilometres up the coast to the naval base near Shelburne, where it was joined by a second craft before both flew away.

163
00:10:56,500 --> 00:11:02,606
Shelburne was a real US-linked sonar station, and that sounds convincing.

164
00:11:02,606 --> 00:11:09,466
But nothing in the 1967 files, no ship's log, no radar record, mentions any of it.

165
00:11:09,933 --> 00:11:14,255
The new material comes from witnesses recalled decades later.

166
00:11:14,255 --> 00:11:19,073
Memory does what the telex did: it smooths, adds and fills the gaps.

167
00:11:19,073 --> 00:11:23,466
Without a record from the time, that is a story, not evidence.

168
00:11:23,933 --> 00:11:26,517
What about a secret military test?

169
00:11:26,517 --> 00:11:28,417
It is a popular fallback.

170
00:11:28,417 --> 00:11:36,321
But secret aircraft are tested in remote ranges, not low over a village with a dozen witnesses watching.

171
00:11:36,321 --> 00:11:39,133
Skeptoid calls it logically excluded.

172
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,049
Today the village keeps the memory.

173
00:11:42,049 --> 00:11:45,268
A lookout over the bay and a small UFO centre.

174
00:11:45,268 --> 00:11:49,466
For Shag Harbour, the mystery became a part of its identity.

175
00:11:49,933 --> 00:11:50,474
Debrief.

176
00:11:50,474 --> 00:11:52,299
Time to score Shag Harbour.

177
00:11:52,766 --> 00:11:58,102
Several independent witnesses saw lights descend toward the water: evidence.

178
00:11:58,102 --> 00:12:02,314
Police officers saw a light floating, then vanish: evidence.

179
00:12:02,314 --> 00:12:06,877
The foam as a physical trace: partial, since it was never tested.

180
00:12:06,877 --> 00:12:09,966
A craft or wreckage found by the navy: none.

181
00:12:10,433 --> 00:12:18,195
The claim that the government ruled out flares: debunked, it came from a loose summary of a nil-results telex.

182
00:12:18,195 --> 00:12:22,147
A craft travelling underwater to Shelburne: unsupported.

183
00:12:22,147 --> 00:12:24,899
An extraterrestrial craft: no evidence.

184
00:12:25,366 --> 00:12:26,269
The verdict.

185
00:12:26,269 --> 00:12:28,979
Real event, unidentified, not alien.

186
00:12:28,979 --> 00:12:37,636
Honest people saw lights come down, police saw one float, and Canada's military took it seriously enough to search.

187
00:12:37,636 --> 00:12:41,399
A fireball or flares remain the likeliest answers.

188
00:12:41,866 --> 00:12:43,902
And the lesson is the relay.

189
00:12:43,902 --> 00:12:47,465
The core of the story survived four links intact.

190
00:12:47,465 --> 00:12:53,500
What did not survive was the memo's meaning, the foam's size, and the second craft.

191
00:12:53,966 --> 00:12:58,498
Next case: December nineteen sixty-five, Kecksburg, Pennsylvania.

192
00:12:58,498 --> 00:13:05,329
Witnesses saw a fireball fall into the woods, and reports spoke of something shaped like an acorn.

193
00:13:05,329 --> 00:13:06,933
Was anything recovered?

194
00:13:07,399 --> 00:13:10,058
What do you think came down at Shag Harbour?

195
00:13:10,058 --> 00:13:15,133
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
